      "id": "s1",
      "nodeId": "n3",
      "text": "Army Field Manual used to claim \"40 to 45 percent of body heat\" is lost through the head, but it is simply not true, according to the British Medical Journal.",
      "contextBefore": "rce on Google Subscribe to our newsletter How special is the human head compared with other body parts when it comes to losing or retaining body heat? As it turns out, not that special. Even the U.S. ",
      "contextAfter": " This heat-loss myth probably came from experiments in the 1950s, when military researchers exposed subjects to frigid temperatures. While their bodies were bundled up, their heads were exposed - and ",
      "start": 3170,
      "end": 3328,
      "extractSha256": "e9c7906b18c6fbc4060f56d3cdedd6da9b44ceafec8401a0cd84fa375c6b7c61",
      "windowSha256": "a43dc6fb021e2d0c7ebefa9b92a2a255285a74e796336214441cc090db9bcf00",
      "fetchedAt": "2026-11-18T02:59:24.198Z",
      "waybackUrl": "https://web.archive.org/web/20130703051648/https://www.livescience.com/34411-body-heat-loss-head.html",
      "tier": "exact"
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "nodeId": "n4",
      "text": "This idea was then perpetuated by a 1970s edition of a US Army survival field guide that recommended covering one's head in frigid temperatures because \"40 to 45 percent of body heat\" is lost through the head.",
      "contextBefore": "atures while wearing arctic survival suits. However, the suits only covered the subjects from the neck down. Therefore, naturally, the majority of the heat loss occurred by way of the uncovered head. ",
      "contextAfter": " Since heat loss from any body region is largely dependent upon surface area, you can see why this belief isn't logical, because your head comprises only about 10% of your body's total surface area. T",
      "start": 895,
      "end": 1104,
      "extractSha256": "0038b773acfd12cb96ea7b5ee938375d386ea12fd31b4be077611e49a9995c74",
      "windowSha256": "085dcf14331f766ce638b5be9b20ce28bc6608d94112fea0115f12c20c76f349",
      "fetchedAt": "2026-11-18T02:59:25.223Z",
      "waybackUrl": "https://web.archive.org/web/20210420164813/https://www.onemedical.com/blog/healthy-living/body-heat",
      "tier": "exact"
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "nodeId": "n5",
      "text": "Army Field Manual claims \"40 to 45 percent of body heat\" is lost through the head, the researchers write in this week's article, but it is simply not true.",
      "contextBefore": "o call the poison control center when any non-food plant is eaten, she said. Myth 4: You lose most of your body heat through your head. Your mother said it. Every hat salesman touts it. Even the U.S. ",
      "contextAfter": " Body heat leaves from any skin surface in proportion to the area exposed, said Vreeman. As for people who claim a hat renders shorts acceptable in cold weather? \"Those people are being very, very foo",
      "start": 5389,
